# ⚠️ LOAD BEARING. Do not remove, and do not "tidy" these to text=auto.
#
# A mail message on the wire uses CRLF, DKIM signs the body as CRLF, and a strict reading of RFC 6376
# relaxed canonicalization splits the body on CRLF. Git's core.autocrlf=input, which is a common and
# otherwise sensible setting, converts these files to bare LF on commit, so the bytes stored are not
# the bytes that were signed and what a clone checks out depends on the cloner's git config.
#
# Measured on the first commit of this repo, which is how it was caught:
#
#   working copy, CRLF      body hash matches the bh= in the signature
#   after git conversion    body hash MISMATCHES under a strict canonicalizer
#
# dkimpy happens to verify BOTH, because it normalizes line endings before canonicalizing, and that
# tolerance is exactly what makes this dangerous rather than obvious. The corpus exists to be the
# reference a verifier is measured against, and a fixture whose verdict depends on how lenient the
# reader is cannot measure anything. A strict verifier would report a valid signature as broken and
# the fault would look like a bug in the verifier.
#
# -text means git stores and checks out these bytes exactly as they are, on every platform.
*.eml -text

# The zone files and the policy file are read by servers that are relaxed about line endings, but
# there is no reason for them to vary either.
*.txt text eol=lf
